      <h2 id="evidence">The evidence, briefly</h2>
      <div class="call evidence">
        <div class="h">&#128218; What research supports</div>
        <p>Frequent sauna use is linked to lower cardiovascular and all-cause mortality in large Finnish cohort data (Laukkanen et al., <em>JAMA Internal Medicine</em>, 2015). Cold-water immersion reliably raises norepinephrine and dopamine (Šrámek et al., 2000) and is tied to brown-fat activation (Søberg et al., <em>Cell Reports Medicine</em>, 2021). Contrast water therapy reduces exercise-induced soreness in recovery meta-analyses.</p>
      </div>
      <p><strong>The caveat worth repeating:</strong> most rigorous studies isolate heat <em>or</em> cold; controlled trials on the exact sauna-then-plunge combination are still limited, and much of the data is observational. The individual benefits are well supported — treat the combined ritual as a smart, enjoyable way to get both.</p>

      <div class="call safety" id="safety">
        <div class="h">&#9888; Safety first</div>
        <p>Check with your doctor before starting if you have heart disease, high or low blood pressure, are pregnant, or have conditions like epilepsy or Raynaud's. Never plunge alone or after drinking, ease in gradually, and exit if you feel faint, dizzy, or disoriented. This article is educational and not medical advice.</p>
      </div>

