          <li>Šrámek P, et al. Human physiological responses to immersion into water of different temperatures. <em>European Journal of Applied Physiology</em>, 2000.</li>
          <li>Søberg S, et al. Cold-induced thermogenesis and brown adipose tissue in winter swimmers. <em>Cell Reports Medicine</em>, 2021.</li>
          <li>Bieuzen F, et al. Contrast water therapy and exercise-induced muscle damage: a systematic review. <em>PLoS ONE</em>, 2013.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <details class="faq"><summary>What is contrast therapy?</summary><div class="a"><p>Contrast therapy is alternating heat (like a sauna) and cold (like a cold plunge) in the same session — typically several rounds — to trigger a vascular pump and a mix of recovery, mood, and resilience benefits.</p></div></details>
      <details class="faq"><summary>What are the benefits of contrast therapy?</summary><div class="a"><p>The most-cited benefits are faster workout recovery, improved mood and energy, better sleep, boosted circulation, stress resilience, and modest metabolic effects. Evidence is strongest for the individual heat and cold components.</p></div></details>
      <details class="faq"><summary>How do I start contrast therapy as a beginner?</summary><div class="a"><p>Sauna ~10 minutes, then a 30&ndash;60 second cold plunge at a gentle 55&ndash;60&deg;F, rest, and repeat once. Do two rounds, two times in your first week, and build cold duration and rounds gradually over a month.</p></div></details>
      <details class="faq"><summary>How often should I do contrast therapy?</summary><div class="a"><p>2&ndash;4 sessions per week works for most people, with 1&ndash;2 full rest days. A popular weekly target is about 11 minutes of total cold and roughly an hour of heat, split across sessions.</p></div></details>
      <details class="faq"><summary>Do I need special equipment to start?</summary><div class="a"><p>No. The easiest start is a studio that has both a sauna and a cold plunge. At home you can begin with a basic ice barrel and even a cold shower before investing in a chiller tub or sauna cabin.</p></div></details>
      <details class="faq"><summary>Can I do contrast therapy at home?</summary><div class="a"><p>Yes. Many people run the full protocol at home with an infrared sauna (or blanket) plus a cold plunge tub or ice bath. Just apply the same safety rules: heat first, never plunge alone or impaired, and build up gradually.</p></div></details>
      <details class="faq"><summary>Is contrast therapy safe?</summary><div class="a"><p>For most healthy adults, yes, when done sensibly. But heat and cold stress the cardiovascular system, so anyone with heart conditions, blood-pressure issues, pregnancy, or related concerns should consult a doctor first and never practice alone or after alcohol.</p></div></details>

      <div class="finale">
        <h2>Ready for round one?</h2>
        <p>The best way to learn contrast therapy is to feel it. Find a studio with a sauna and a cold plunge near you on ContrastMap — and start the cycle.</p>
